#warning-env(title: [Commercial Microaggregate Testing Is Not Supported by the Evidence])[
Private laboratories sell microaggregate or microclot testing, usually bundled with a protocol that includes anticoagulation, fibrinolytic enzymes, or apheresis. Nothing in the evidence base supports that package, and the null result removes its main premise.

Three findings converge. The assay has no external reference standard, particles are present in healthy and diabetic plasma as well as post-COVID plasma, several studies scored particles visually without blinding, and the term itself is judged incorrect because the particles are amyloid aggregates rather than thrombi @Cochrane2023Plasmapheresis. A well-controlled prospective cohort found microaggregate number, area, and fibrin fibre density comparable to recovered controls @Fraser2026LCcoagulation. The one large randomised test of an anticoagulant in this population showed no fatigue benefit @StimulateICP2026rivaroxaban.

A patient who pays for this test therefore receives a result that cannot be interpreted against a reference range, cannot be compared with another laboratory's result, and does not identify a treatment that has been shown to help. The downstream risk is not only financial: anticoagulation carries bleeding risk, and fibrinolytic enzyme supplements carry bleeding risk with no controlled efficacy data in ME/CFS.

This is a warning about the test and the protocol, not about the patients who have used them. Patients who pursued microclot testing were usually trying to explain a disabling illness that mainstream medicine had failed to treat, and the failure to offer anything better is part of why these services find a market.

*Consequence:* Patients should not pay for microaggregate testing, and clinicians should not order it or act on its result. A positive result is not a reason to start anticoagulation or a fibrinolytic supplement, and a negative result does not exclude ME/CFS. (Origin: brainstorm.)
] <warn:microclot-testing-commercial-harm>
